{"kind":"propose","text":"Turn the top bar's \"N for you\" counter into a notification list that hangs off your own avatar.\n\nHugh asked for this in session on 2026-08-22, in these terms: the counter is really a mentions and notifications count; what it does when clicked is not obvious, because it takes you to the first of them; it would be better as a list of notifications that are each clickable, shown in a dropdown when you click your own picture, with a bell on the picture when there are notifications. I am filing it as a proposal rather than a request because requester and performer would otherwise be the same actor, which this repository does not allow; ratify this and the implementing commit has a decision to rest on.\n\nWHAT CHANGES. The standalone \"N for you\" button goes. Your own identity chip in the top bar becomes the notification control: it carries a bell badge with the count while anything is unread, and clicking it opens a panel listing every act addressed to you — a request put to you, or a mention of you. Each row names who sent it, whether they asked you or mentioned you, the record's kind and ticket, its title and its time, and clicking a row opens that record. A \"mark all read\" action clears the list without navigating anywhere. The panel closes on Escape, on a click outside it, and on choosing a row. What counts as a notification does not change at all; only how it is presented.\n\nONE SUBSTANTIVE CONSEQUENCE, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH RATIFYING RATHER THAN JUST DOING. The old counter stored a single watermark and advanced it one act per click, and the code says why: each click advances one act, so nothing addressed to you can be skipped unseen. A list breaks that. If you can see five notifications and you open the third, a single watermark buries the first two as a side effect of a click you made about something else. I did not want to drop a stated safety property quietly, so the read position now records a watermark plus any tickets read out of order above it, and collapses a contiguous run back into the watermark so storage stays small. Opening one notification marks exactly that one read. The old bare-number format still loads, as a watermark with nothing above it.\n\nThe invariant the old design protected is still honoured, by a different mechanism: nothing addressed to you is hidden behind a number, because the list shows all of it and you choose what to open.\n\nSCOPE. Browser presentation only. No durable act, no server behaviour, no kernel or fold change; the notification rule itself is untouched. Files: ui/src/components/TopBar.tsx, ui/src/lib/store.ts, ui/src/lib/memory.ts, ui/test/dom.test.mjs, and the regenerated internal/service/uidist embed.","body":{"area":"ui","to":"hugh"}}